WEBVTT

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:07.820
Hi, this is Dr. Jelena Trajkovic, CECS Department, Cal State Long Beach. Let us look into two new

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:15.480
constructs that are always and case statement in this video. So first let's

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:22.340
look at the so-called always block. Always block is going to appear inside of

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:28.620
your module and you can think of it as a black box and that black box is going

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:31.940
to have procedural statements inside it.

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:36.260
We will talk about procedural statement in a little while.

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:40.100
Always blocks are synthesizable constructs.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:44.100
So we need to make sure that whatever we are writing there

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:49.100
is along lines of synthesizable code.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:52.020
And we are going to learn about that

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:53.860
as we move through the course.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.140
So if you look at the always block

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:03.550
On the left-hand side here, you have a syntax of always.

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:08.190
So you have keyword always in sign at,

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:10.510
and there is open and close parentheses

00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:12.110
and there is sensitivity list.

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:16.110
We're going to come to sensitivity list in a little while.

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:19.590
In case always block has more than one statement,

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:23.870
we will need to use begin and end construct.

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:26.990
Right after begin construct, we need to,

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:29.790
we actually have an option to put

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:33.590
declaration for local variables.

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:35.630
And after that, there's going to be

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:39.930
a series of procedural statements.

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:43.830
So far, we have been talking only about

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:45.550
combinational circuits.

00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:48.190
And always statements and always blocks

00:01:48.190 --> 00:01:51.630
are applicable for combinational circuits.

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:53.790
What goes into sensitivity list

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:55.370
for the combinational circuit?

00:01:55.370 --> 00:02:00.370
Let's look at that in an example of multiplexer

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:02.350
on the right hand side.

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:04.530
So now question is,

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:09.530
when do we want value of M out

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:13.410
out of the multiplexer to change?

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:19.650
Do we want it to change when value of I0 or I1 changes?

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:29.570
of I0 or I1 changes? Do we want it to change when value of S0 changes? Or do we need to take into

00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:41.070
account all of the inputs? So we do need to take into account all the inputs. Basically, when any

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:51.470
of the input values changes, it doesn't matter if they are so-called data inputs as I0 and I1,

00:02:51.470 --> 00:03:03.070
or control inputs as a select signal, all of that has to trigger our execution of our always block.

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:13.390
So, insensitivity list. Right now, for educational purpose, we are going to list all the inputs.

00:03:13.390 --> 00:03:25.950
So, you're going to read this as, okay, always IN0, IN1, SEL.

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:37.430
So that means always if IN0, or if IN1, or SEL changes its value, the statements inside

00:03:37.430 --> 00:03:41.430
of the always block are going to execute.

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:44.310
Now, there is a little disclaimer here.

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:50.030
This is very traditional and somewhat dated way

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:51.870
to write the code.

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:56.870
Because listing all the inputs might be error prone,

00:03:57.470 --> 00:04:01.870
right now industrial standard in practice

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:06.430
is for combinational logic to use always at

00:04:06.430 --> 00:04:08.310
and then open and close parentheses

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:10.470
and there is a little asterisk.

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:15.470
However, because we are novices in this

00:04:17.030 --> 00:04:20.630
and we have never seen always statement before,

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:24.270
for a little while we will be listing all the inputs.

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:28.050
So just that you understand that this statement

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:32.290
is going to be quote unquote triggered

00:04:32.290 --> 00:04:37.290
by a change of value in any of the variables

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:42.290
any of the variables that are listed in the list explicitly.

00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:48.310
We will be using also the asterisks later on in the course.

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:54.050
Now let's look into the case statement.

00:04:55.250 --> 00:04:57.230
First of all, case statement

00:04:57.230 --> 00:05:00.610
is one of the procedural statements.

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:05.150
And it is always going to appear inside of always walk.

00:05:05.150 --> 00:05:08.230
So that is a very important thing

00:05:08.230 --> 00:05:12.490
that all procedural statements are inside of always blocks.

00:05:12.490 --> 00:05:14.670
With one more exception,

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:18.890
they can also appear inside of initial,

00:05:18.890 --> 00:05:21.990
initial begin end that we have in test benches.

00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:26.090
You will, those are also procedural statements.

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:29.790
There is procedural assignments

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:34.230
that we are going to talk about in the next slide.

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:35.070
Watch out.

00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:40.470
So there are procedural statements and one variety of them are assignment, procedural

00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:41.470
assignment.

00:05:41.470 --> 00:05:46.830
If statements, to be also talked about later, not in this video, but afterwards, some of

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:47.830
the later ones.

00:05:47.830 --> 00:05:52.690
And then we have case statements and that's where we are at.

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:58.790
So case statement is very similar to a switch statement in traditional programming language.

00:05:58.790 --> 00:06:06.550
have a case word case, and then in parentheses you have an expression that you need to evaluate.

00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:14.230
And then we list possibilities that for this expression. So we list item one,

00:06:15.270 --> 00:06:21.910
item two, item three, and so on and so forth. If we have only one procedural statement, we will

00:06:21.910 --> 00:06:27.910
not need begin and end, but if we have more than one statement, we will have after item

00:06:27.910 --> 00:06:32.950
0, here we will have begin, list of procedural statement end, and so on and so forth.

00:06:33.910 --> 00:06:41.110
And in the end we have end case. So what is going to happen? We are going to take this case

00:06:41.110 --> 00:06:47.830
expression and we are going to evaluate it. And we are going to see if this case expression

00:06:47.830 --> 00:06:54.790
is equal to item 0. Let's say it is not equal to item 0. Then we are going to move on to item 1.

00:06:54.790 --> 00:07:04.430
1. Is it equal to item 1? Let's say no, it's not. Then we move to so and so forth until

00:07:04.430 --> 00:07:13.350
we come to the time that it is equal to item n. When it is equal to item n, we go into

00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:22.830
the body of that begin to end portion. We execute that. Once we hit that end of statement

00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:26.230
of item M, we're done.

00:07:26.230 --> 00:07:32.990
We go to end case and we go outside of this code.

00:07:33.030 --> 00:07:34.670
We are done with case statement.

00:07:34.670 --> 00:07:38.070
We are going to sit and wait for always statement

00:07:38.070 --> 00:07:42.070
to have something in its sensitivity list,

00:07:42.070 --> 00:07:45.510
change its value so that we can reevaluate

00:07:45.510 --> 00:07:46.990
or re-execute this code.

00:07:48.270 --> 00:07:52.270
There are two things that we need to understand here.

00:07:52.270 --> 00:07:59.070
understand here. First thing is that order of listings of item 0, item 1, item 2 is

00:07:59.070 --> 00:08:07.710
important. So, because if we match case expression to item 0, item 0 is going to

00:08:07.710 --> 00:08:14.110
execute. We are going to hit the end here. We are going to fly out of case statement.

00:08:14.110 --> 00:08:19.270
We are done. So, order is important. There is to some extent some priority

00:08:19.270 --> 00:08:26.190
into this. The second thing is, at the bottom there is something called the default and it is

00:08:26.190 --> 00:08:33.710
prudent to have default keys because that one is going to quote-unquote catch all the errors

00:08:33.710 --> 00:08:40.870
and unexpected behavior and that's going to signal to you that something is off. This default

00:08:40.870 --> 00:08:50.230
is really used for for simulation and checking things in simulation. So this is

00:08:50.230 --> 00:08:56.470
what the case statement looks like and how it works. The example of case

00:08:56.470 --> 00:09:04.870
statement is going to be our 2 to 1 multiplexer. So so far we have described

00:09:04.870 --> 00:09:11.110
structure. We used individual gates or groups of gates, we connected them and we

00:09:11.110 --> 00:09:17.230
made some function. Here we are going to describe a multiplexer in terms of

00:09:17.230 --> 00:09:24.710
behavior, basically how it functions. We are not going to go into what is its

00:09:24.710 --> 00:09:29.790
structure or what elements are inside of it. Although I know that you know that

00:09:29.790 --> 00:09:37.910
already because we've done that in class. So the way things are, if you look at

00:09:37.910 --> 00:09:44.350
the description of multiplexer, basically model of multiplexer using Verilog, is such

00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:50.690
that it has two inputs that are IN0 and IN1. These are quote-unquote data

00:09:50.690 --> 00:09:55.990
inputs, it has a select input and then it has one output. We have to put it as

00:09:55.990 --> 00:10:07.910
as REG register, because we will be manipulate it inside of the always statement. So we have

00:10:07.910 --> 00:10:15.190
to do a procedural assignment to that value and because of that it needs to be REG.

00:10:15.190 --> 00:10:21.510
So now we go into the body of this module. We have always statement and it is telling

00:10:21.510 --> 00:10:29.990
us if the value of IN0, IN1, or SEL change, we will go and execute and re-evaluate this

00:10:29.990 --> 00:10:39.430
SEL statement. Sorry, this case statement for variable SEL. So we will take SEL and compare.

00:10:39.430 --> 00:10:47.110
Is SEL equal to 0? If it is, this line is going to be executed. If it is equal to 1,

00:10:47.110 --> 00:10:50.150
this line is going to be executed.

00:10:50.150 --> 00:10:55.150
However, if SEL is not quite either one of them,

00:10:55.750 --> 00:10:58.030
we are going to have some default value.

00:10:58.030 --> 00:11:03.030
It's good to give something that is really, really,

00:11:03.110 --> 00:11:06.990
very funky, like this one bit Z,

00:11:06.990 --> 00:11:08.670
and this is high impedance,

00:11:09.870 --> 00:11:11.830
because that is going to tell us,

00:11:11.830 --> 00:11:13.470
hey, something went wrong.

00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:19.890
There is a good chance that you didn't connect something properly when you are instantiating

00:11:19.890 --> 00:11:30.310
your module, because SEL is always going to have either value of zero or value of one.

00:11:30.310 --> 00:11:35.790
And if we do not get value of zero and value of one, either we did not collect something

00:11:35.790 --> 00:11:41.110
properly or we didn't even specify the value of SEL in our test bench.

00:11:41.110 --> 00:11:48.230
So that is a good indicator that something is abnormal and it needs to have a little

00:11:48.230 --> 00:11:52.390
bit of our attention.

00:11:52.390 --> 00:12:00.150
It is going to be good that you peek into the way that this module is synthesized just

00:12:00.150 --> 00:12:05.150
for fun of it to see what does tool see

00:12:06.190 --> 00:12:11.190
when we describe a module using how we want it to function

00:12:13.030 --> 00:12:17.350
rather than how we really want it to implement

00:12:17.350 --> 00:12:20.910
using gates and connections within gates.

00:12:22.470 --> 00:12:24.750
So last but not least,

00:12:24.750 --> 00:12:29.750
one of the procedural statements is a procedural assignment.

00:12:29.750 --> 00:12:34.750
And as I mentioned, it is inside initial,

00:12:34.770 --> 00:12:37.550
but it's inside of always block.

00:12:38.470 --> 00:12:42.190
And if you remember from previous videos,

00:12:42.190 --> 00:12:46.510
when we have modules and assigned statements,

00:12:46.510 --> 00:12:48.590
outside of this always blocks,

00:12:48.590 --> 00:12:53.030
things always execute concurrently.

00:12:53.030 --> 00:12:58.030
However, the moment we put this procedural assignment

00:12:58.030 --> 00:13:06.990
assignment inside always block or inside initial block, the code executes sequentially. And that

00:13:06.990 --> 00:13:17.310
is a big, big, big difference. Now, within that procedural assignment statements, we have two

00:13:17.310 --> 00:13:27.310
options. One looks like regular equal. That one is called walking. And the other one looks like

00:13:28.030 --> 00:13:33.030
of what you would write in regular programming languages

00:13:34.110 --> 00:13:35.950
as less than equal.

00:13:35.950 --> 00:13:37.070
This is not less than equal.

00:13:37.070 --> 00:13:39.750
This is so-called non-blocking statement.

00:13:39.750 --> 00:13:44.270
So let's go in order and they appear.

00:13:44.270 --> 00:13:46.670
So we have variable name and we have,

00:13:46.670 --> 00:13:49.950
that variable is going to get the expression,

00:13:49.950 --> 00:13:53.070
the value of the expression on the right-hand side.

00:13:53.070 --> 00:13:54.670
What does it mean, blocking?

00:13:54.670 --> 00:14:03.390
that the variable of the life-sense side is going to have a value of the expression immediately.

00:14:03.390 --> 00:14:09.710
We are going sequentially, we are going line by line in the code, we are going to assign

00:14:09.710 --> 00:14:17.870
this the moment we execute this statement, and before we go to next statement, the value

00:14:17.870 --> 00:14:19.910
is going to be updated.

00:14:19.910 --> 00:14:25.110
that would mean it means blocking. So we are not going to, we are blocked on this statement

00:14:25.110 --> 00:14:34.950
until the assignment happens. And that is actually how combinational circuits work.

00:14:34.950 --> 00:14:43.550
Therefore, the blocking statement should be used for combinational circuits. That is

00:14:43.550 --> 00:14:45.750
what it's meant.

00:14:45.750 --> 00:14:52.710
Now question is, because these are inside of always statement,

00:14:52.710 --> 00:14:55.630
what goes into a sensitivity list?

00:14:55.630 --> 00:15:00.590
Now pause and go back if you didn't miss that in this video.

00:15:00.590 --> 00:15:06.510
We have to do either spelled correctly, all inputs,

00:15:06.510 --> 00:15:11.790
or we have to do a little asterisk in those parentheses.

00:15:11.790 --> 00:15:13.550
So that's very important.

00:15:13.550 --> 00:15:17.190
The second flavor of this procedural assignment

00:15:17.190 --> 00:15:19.310
is so-called non-blocking.

00:15:19.310 --> 00:15:24.310
And it has different sign for it,

00:15:25.590 --> 00:15:28.070
which just looks like less than equal.

00:15:28.070 --> 00:15:31.470
So variable is going to get assigned

00:15:31.470 --> 00:15:34.430
the value of expression on the right-hand side,

00:15:34.430 --> 00:15:37.810
only at the end of the always block.

00:15:38.990 --> 00:15:43.390
So that is what is characteristic of sequential code

00:15:43.390 --> 00:15:49.270
sequential code, we are going to start in a little while. We still have quite a few

00:15:49.270 --> 00:15:56.350
of combinational circuits to cover. So therefore, non-blocking assignment should be used for

00:15:56.350 --> 00:16:03.350
sequential circuits. Sometimes it may be used for combinational circuits. In sometimes cases

00:16:03.350 --> 00:16:10.830
when we have insane always block to deal with both sequential and combinational logic.

00:16:10.830 --> 00:16:17.670
And I do not think that we will have that date case in this course.

00:16:17.670 --> 00:16:20.310
What goes into sensitivity list?

00:16:20.310 --> 00:16:27.310
Well, clock and reset and very often positive edge of clock and reset.

00:16:27.310 --> 00:16:34.470
That is topic for our sequential code and for modeling of sequential code and sequential

00:16:34.470 --> 00:16:35.470
circuits.

00:16:35.470 --> 00:16:38.430
Sorry, not sequential code, sequential circuits.

00:16:38.430 --> 00:16:39.430
I hope this was helpful.

00:16:39.430 --> 00:16:40.030
Thank you.

